 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Block programming (for example Scratch) builds programs by snapping together coloured blocks instead of typing code.\nKey ideas:\n• An event block starts the program (“when green flag clicked”).\n• A sequence is a list of instructions done one after another, in order.\n• A sprite is a character that the blocks control; the stage is its background.\n• Motion blocks move the sprite (“move 10 steps”, “turn 90 degrees”); looks blocks change its look (“say Hello”).\n• A loop repeats blocks: “repeat 4” repeats a fixed number of times; “forever” repeats without end.\n• A variable stores a value that can change (“set score to 0”, “change score by 1”).\nExample: repeat 4 [move 100 steps, turn 90 degrees] draws a square.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": 2,
   "b": 3,
   "n": 4
  },
  "problem": "A program does: set x to {a}; repeat {n} times: change x by {b}. What is x at the end?",
  "steps": [
   "Each time round the loop, x increases by {b}.",
   "After {n} times: {a} + {b} × {n} = {traceAdd(a, b, n)}."
  ],
  "answer": "{traceAdd(a, b, n)}",
  "checks": []
 },
